#shell features

#shell functions
#shell functions allow us to group commands for later execution using a single name for the group. They are executed
#just like a simple command. When using shell function names as a simple command name, the shelle xecutes the list 
#of commands associated with that function name. Shell functions are executed in the current shell context, no new
# process is created to interpret them.
#function declaration
fname () compound-command [ redirection ]
or
function fname [()] compound-command [ redirection ]
#this defines a shell function called fname; function reserved word is optional, but including it makes the 
#parenthesis optional. The body of the function is the compound command compound-command. This is usually a list 
#enclosed between braces {}, but can be any compound command listed above. Using the function reserved word and not
# providing parenthesis, then it is recommended to use braces. When shell is in POSIX mode, fname must be a valid 
#shell name and can not be the same as one of the special builtins. When not in POSIX mode, then a function name
#can be any unquoted shell word that does not contain $.
#Any redirections associated with the shell function are performed when the function is executed. Function 
#definitions are deleted using the -f option to the unset builtin. 
#The exit status of a function definition is zero unless a syntax error occurs or a deadonly function witht he same
# name already exists. The exit status of the function when executed is the exit status of the last command
#executed in the body.
#for historical reasons, in the most common usage the curly braces that surround the body of the function must be
# separated from the body by blanks or newlines. This is because the braces are reserved words and are only 
#recognised as such when separated from the command list by whitespace or other shell metacharacters. Also, 
#when suing braces, the list must be terminated with a semicolon, &, or a newline
#compound-command is executed whenever fname is specified as the name of a simple command. Functions are executed 
#in the context of the calling shell; no new peocess is created to interpret them.
#When executing a function, the arguments to the function become the positional parameters during its execution.
#The special parameter # that expands to the number of positional parameters is updated to reflect the new set of
#positional parameters. Special parameter 0 is uncanged. The first element of the FUNCNAME variable is set to the
#name of the function while the function is executing.
#All other aspects of shell execution environemnt are identical between a function and its caller apart from: the
# DEBUG and RETURN traps are not inherited unless the function has been given the trace builtin attribute using
#the declare builtin or the -o functrace option has been enabled with the set builtin , and the ERR trap is not 
#inherited unless the -o errtrace shell option has been enabled. 
#The FUNCNEST variable, if set to a numeric value greater than 0, defines a maximum function nesting level. 
#Function invocations that exceed the limit cause the entire command to abort.
#If the builtin command RETUN is executed in a function, then the function completes and execution resumes with the
# next command after the function call. Also, any command associated with the RETURN trap is executed before 
#execution resumes. When a function completes, the values of the positional parameters and the special parameter #
#are restored to the values they had before the function's execution. If return ha sa numeric argument, that is the
# function's return status; otherwise the function's return status is the exit status of the last command executed
# before the return.  
#Variables local to a function are declared witht he local builtin. Ordinarily, variables and their values are shared
#between a function and its caller. These variables are visible only to the function and the commands it invokes. 
#This is particularly important when a shell function calls other functions.
#Current scope is a currently executing function. Previous scopes refer to the caller, repeating until the global 
#scope is reached, where the shell is not executing a shell function. Local variables in the current local 
#scope are declared using the local or declare builtins in the function that is currently executing.
#Local variables shadow variables of the same name declared in previous scopes (including global variables).
#References and assignments thus would only refer to the local variable, not the one from the previous scope.
#shell uses dynamic scoping.
#For example, if a variable var is declared as local in function func1, and func1 calls another function func2, references to var made from within func2 resolve to the local variable var from func1, shadowing any global 
#variable named var.
func1() 
{
   local var='func1 local'
   func2
}

func2()
{
   echo "in func2, var = $var"
}
var=global
func1
